diff --git a/apps/desktop/README.md b/apps/desktop/README.md
index b5967d94..eeb2ff84 100644
--- a/apps/desktop/README.md
+++ b/apps/desktop/README.md
@@ -56,11 +56,13 @@ Press F12 (Fn+F12 on media-key keyboards), Command+Option+I on macOS, or Ctrl+Sh
 
 ## Terminal command
 
-The application menu's **Manage dsh Command…** entry, immediately below **Check for Updates…**, shows the current command and offers Install, Repair, and Remove. The command uses Desktop's installed runtime and the ordinary [dsh CLI](../cli/README.md), including when the Desktop application is closed. Open a new terminal after installation and run `dsh --version`.
+The application menu's **Install Command-Line Tool…** entry, immediately below **Check for Updates…**, shows the current command and offers Install, Repair, and Remove. The `harness-studio` command uses Desktop's installed runtime and the ordinary [dsh CLI](../cli/README.md), including when the Desktop application is closed. Open a new terminal after installation and run `harness-studio --version`.
 
-On macOS, installation creates `/usr/local/bin/dsh` and requests administrator authentication when the directory requires it. Shell startup files are unchanged. On Windows, the management dialog registers the command in the current user's PATH. A pre-existing command requires confirmation before switching; repairing Desktop's own selected command does not repeat that confirmation. The macOS link preserves and restores the displaced launcher; Windows preserves other PATH entries, including entries that predate registration. A command with higher PATH precedence is reported with its location. After moving the application, use Repair on macOS or Install from the new location on Windows. Remove leaves unrelated installations intact.
+The launcher sets `DSH_HOME` to `~/.harness-studio` unless it is already set, so the command shares the application's settings, saved model key and sessions. A first argument that is not a launcher mode, flag or profile name (`web`, `plugin`, `acp`, `headless`, `sdk`, `sdk-minimal`, `desktop`, `harness-studio`, `--profile`, `--from-default-profile`, a config dump, `--version` or `--help`), or no argument at all, selects the `harness-studio` profile: one headless task (from the argument, or from stdin when there is none) with `@harness-studio/connectors`, `@harness-studio/remember`, `@harness-studio/hooks` and `@harness-studio/compact` over `dsh-base`. Compact's host half carries its site approval there; with no approval card to show, a web fetch or page to a site the network setting does not allow is refused. Its other flags, such as `--json` and `--session-id`, are the [headless runner's](../../packages/bundle/headless/README.md).
 
-Command registration is optional after installing Desktop. Use **Manage dsh Command… → Remove** before uninstalling Desktop to remove its CLI registration; the application uninstaller does not remove it. Finish CLI commands before updating or uninstalling Desktop. The CLI runtime version follows the installed Desktop release. See [bundled command runtime](#bundled-command-runtime) for Desktop plugin commands and runtime limitations.
+On macOS, installation creates `/usr/local/bin/harness-studio` and requests administrator authentication when the directory requires it. Shell startup files are unchanged. On Windows, the management dialog registers the command in the current user's PATH. A pre-existing command requires confirmation before switching; repairing Desktop's own selected command does not repeat that confirmation. The macOS link preserves and restores the displaced launcher; Windows preserves other PATH entries, including entries that predate registration. A command with higher PATH precedence is reported with its location. After moving the application, use Repair on macOS or Install from the new location on Windows. Remove leaves unrelated installations intact.
+
+Command registration is optional after installing Desktop. Use **Install Command-Line Tool… → Remove** before uninstalling Desktop to remove its CLI registration; the application uninstaller does not remove it. Finish CLI commands before updating or uninstalling Desktop. The CLI runtime version follows the installed Desktop release. See [bundled command runtime](#bundled-command-runtime) for Desktop plugin commands and runtime limitations.
 
 <a id="closing-the-window-and-quitting"></a>
 
diff --git a/apps/desktop/cli/dsh b/apps/desktop/cli/dsh
index ee657a3d..765c0d19 100644
--- a/apps/desktop/cli/dsh
+++ b/apps/desktop/cli/dsh
@@ -11,4 +11,14 @@ while [ -L "$launcher" ]; do
   esac
 done
 resources=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$launcher")/../../.." && pwd -P)
-ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 exec "$resources/../MacOS/DeepSeek Harness" --expose-internals "$resources/app.asar/dsh/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh-desktop-host/lib/cli.js" "$@"
+# Harness Studio keeps its settings, keys and sessions in ~/.harness-studio, like the app; an explicit
+# DSH_HOME still wins.
+: "${DSH_HOME:=$HOME/.harness-studio}"
+export DSH_HOME
+# A task with no mode runs one headless session in Harness Studio's command-line profile, which adds its
+# connectors, memory and hooks to the base composition. Launcher modes, flags and named profiles pass through.
+case "${1-}" in
+  web|plugin|acp|headless|sdk|sdk-minimal|desktop|harness-studio|--profile|--profile=*|--from-default-profile|--from-default-profile=*|--dump-config|--dump-default-config|--dump-config-schema|--version|-V|--help|-h) ;;
+  *) set -- --profile harness-studio "$@" ;;
+esac
+ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 exec "$resources/../MacOS/Harness Studio" --expose-internals "$resources/app.asar/dsh/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh-desktop-host/lib/cli.js" "$@"
